ath" "q-37600-after.png" "sha256" "07857ac7078f6b98febef4e88c52d8772a8bc4c9720dbd771755cc5091d22a3e"} {"path" "history-q-37600.json" "sha256" "f66471f6366413e4c9d1bca1da609f246db6c49da0c12ef5e9da72e8acf7cf73"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "cc2d70009abd8cf33e23f293f72bd62b5dabcec9c451b8d659244716f47bffcc"}]} {"question" "q-37600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "17dfb3c43635a4576a076f0bcd777033a65405141eca8b05088c91971e8dc647" "rationale" "Place eigenvalues -3 and 1 on D's diagonal and their respective eigenvectors (2,0) and (-2,1) in P's columns. Then AP=PD and det(P)=2, so PDP^-1=A. Original option d (index 3), the authentic worked solution, and successful grading agree. Stored bmatrix notation and captured square-fenced aligned notation preserve exactly the same entries and row/column positions."}] "usage" {"q-37610" [] "q-37600" [] "e-8572" [] "e-8574" [] "q-37609" [] "e-8573" [] "e-8575" []} "basis" 1419 "retractions" [[17592186125006 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $λ_{1} = 3$ and $λ_{2} = 15$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a, b, c$ given that\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}$?"] [17592186125006 :question/answer-fields 17592186123058] [17592186125003 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$. You're given that $λ_{1} =-3$ and $λ_{2} = 1$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$, $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A$, where $D$ is a diagonal matrix."] [17592186125003 :question/answer-fields 17592186123040] [17592186125004 :question/problem "If the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$."] [17592186125004 :question/answer-fields 17592186123046] [17592186124332 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A$, where $D$ is a diagonal matrix."] [17592186124332 :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nwe construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P$, we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns:\n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$, and\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}$.\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186124329 :question/problem "If the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$."] [17592186124329 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal.\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3\n$$\n\nSince we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 3I &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."] [17592186124330 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP$, find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}$."] [17592186124330 :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nComputing $A-(-1)I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - (-1)I &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} = R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1= -{2}x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy setting $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2\n$$"] [17592186124331 :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP$."] [17592186124331 :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3$, we have\n$A - 3I|= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n$M|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} = R_{2} + (-\\frac{5}{8})R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting $x_1=1$, we get the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2$, we have\n$A - (-2)I|= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} + 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{5}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting $x_2=5$, we get the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123041 17592186123042 17592186123043 17592186123044 17592186123045 17592186123047 17592186123048 17592186123049 17592186123050 17592186123051 17592186123059 17592186123060 17592186123061 17592186123062 17592186123063] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123040 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123040 :answer-field/id] [17592186123040 :answer-field/key] [17592186123040 :answer-field/type] [17592186123046 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123046 :answer-field/id] [17592186123046 :answer-field/key] [17592186123046 :answer-field/type] [17592186123058 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123058 :answer-field/id] [17592186123058 :answer-field/key] [17592186123058 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
